course: dictionary
lesson: 10386
title: Big words, big feelings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Tremendous is a describing word with two main meanings. The first is very great in size, amount or strength, so huge: a tremendous noise is a very loud noise. The second is informal, meaning very good or excellent: a tremendous goal is a brilliant goal.
explain: Look at the words around it to choose the right meaning. Hedgehog says «scales are a tremendous improvement on prickles». Scales are not bigger than prickles, so tremendous here means very good. Ask yourself: is the sentence about size, or about how good something is?
explain: Tremendous appears in stories, sport and science. Toad in The Wind in the Willows loves big words and calls his own adventures tremendous. In science, volcanoes release tremendous energy, a huge amount of energy. The word never gives an exact number. It only tells you that something is very big or very good.
fact: Tremendous means very great or huge, and, in informal speech, excellent.
fact: Tremendous can describe a noise, an effort, a goal or a time.
fact: Informal means relaxed speech and writing, like talking to friends, rather than formal writing.
fact: Hedgehog says «scales are a tremendous improvement on prickles».
fact: Tremendous does not give an exact number; it only says that something is very big or very good.
exercise: «The tremendous noise woke the whole street.» What does tremendous mean here? | Very kind and friendly | Quiet and gentle | Old and worn | *Very loud and huge
exercise: Sam says, «That goal was tremendous!» What does Sam mean? | Very large, a big goal in size | Very slow and boring | *Excellent, a brilliant goal
exercise: «Volcanoes release tremendous energy.» Which word tells you how much energy there is? | release | energy | *tremendous | volcanoes
test: «Hedgehog says scales are a tremendous improvement on prickles.» What does tremendous mean in this sentence? | Very sad | Very great and huge | *Very good, an excellent improvement | Very quiet
test: Toad in The Wind in the Willows calls his own adventures tremendous. What is he saying about them? | Very frightening and dangerous | Very small and quiet | Very tired and slow | *Excellent and thrilling
exam: Which sentence uses tremendous to mean huge in size? | She laughed and said tremendous | The tremendous lesson made me smile | *A tremendous wave crashed over the little boat | Tremendous is my favourite colour
